   "i": 161,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Kitcher and Salmon (1987) were early critics of van Fraassen’s theory of why-questions as a theory of explanation. They object (1987, 319) that the lack of constraints on the relevance relation ⟦M⟧ “allows just about anything to count as the answer to just about any [why-]question.” Other critics of van Fraassen’s theory include Ruben (1987) and Temple (1988), who argue that explanatory contrast is an unnecessary complication because any contrastive why-question ‘why ⟦M⟧ (in contrast to ⟦M⟧ ?’ i",
   "md_text": "Kitcher and Salmon (1987) were early critics of van Fraassen’s theory of why-questions as a theory of explanation. They object (1987, 319) that the lack of constraints on the relevance relation ⟦M⟧ “allows just about anything to count as the answer to just about any [why-]question.” Other critics of van Fraassen’s theory include Ruben (1987) and Temple (1988), who argue that explanatory contrast is an unnecessary complication because any contrastive why-question ‘why ⟦M⟧ (in contrast to ⟦M⟧ ?’ i"
  },
  {
   "i": 39,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Groenendijk and Stokhof (1984) take a question to denote, in each world, a single proposition embodying the true exhaustive answer to the question in that world. For instance, if ⟦M⟧ is a world in which Paul and Nina are coming for dinner, and nobody else is coming, then the denotation of (8) in ⟦M⟧ is the proposition expressed by (8b).",
   "md_text": "Groenendijk and Stokhof (1984) take a question to denote, in each world, a single proposition embodying the true exhaustive answer to the question in that world. For instance, if ⟦M⟧ is a world in which Paul and Nina are coming for dinner, and nobody else is coming, then the denotation of (8) in ⟦M⟧ is the proposition expressed by (8b)."
  },
  {
